# SquirrelJME Usage

This covers all variants of SquirrelJME, for specific variant documentation
please see:

 * [Standalone Usage](usage-standalone.mkd)
 * [SpringCoat Usage](usage-springcoat.mkd)
 * [NanoCoat Usage](usage-nanocoat.mkd)

## Command Line Usage

The following may be used at the command line, the format is the same
as `java`. An option within square brackets `[]` are optional, and
any option within parenthesis `()` are required. The usage of SquirrelJME is
in the following format:

 * `squirreljme [options]`
   * Start into the launcher. 
 * `squirreljme [options] -jar a.jar`
   * Directly launch the given Jar, bypassing the launcher. 
 * `squirreljme [options] mainClass [mainArgs|...]`
   * Standard launch into the specific classpath and main arguments.
   * For this option, `-classpath (class:path:...)` may be used
 * `squirreljme -XdebuggerOnly [options]`
   * Only launch the built-in SquirrelJME debugger and not an actual virtual
     machine.

The available options are:

 * `-Xclutter:(release|debug)`
   * Use the given core libraries with the given clutter level.
   * `release` includes obfuscation and no debugging and thus runs faster.
   * `debug` does not have any obfuscation and may work in cases where
     obfuscation and optimization may cause issues.
 * `-Xdebug[:fork|nofork]`
   * Enable debugging but with the internal built-in SquirrelJME debugger and
     not a JDWP connection over TCP.
   * This is supported under the `hosted` emulator, however if you launch
     another application within it will not debug that process, therefor if
     you wish to debug you should use `-jar`.
   * Optionally, `fork` or `nofork` can be specified to where the
     internal debugger to launched as a separate process or not.
 * `-XdebuggerOnly`
   * Launches the built-in SquirrelJME Debugger, if available. 
 * `-Xemulator:(vm)`
   * Specifies the backend emulator to use, the supported options are:
     * `hosted` -- Uses the host JVM to run all the software.
     * `springcoat` -- SpringCoat, interpreter written in Java.
     * `nanocoat` -- NanoCoat, interpreter written in C.
 * `-Xentry:id`
   * Used with `-jar`, this allows one to specify an entry point index,
     application name, or application entry point to determine which one to
     launch within the Jar as there may be multiple.
   * By default, if not specified, the first is always used.
 * `-Xint`
   * Alias for `-Xemulator:springcoat`.
 * `-Xjdwp:[hostname]:port`
   * Enable debugging.
   * If `hostname` is specified then the debugger will connect to the
     remote host that is a listening JDWP debugger.
   * Otherwise, if only `port` is specified then the debugger will wait and
     listen on the given port for an incoming connection.
 * `-Xlibraries:(class:path:...)`
   * Specifies libraries that should be available to the virtual machine
     and the suite management system, note that this is not the same as
     the `-classpath` option as that is for the _current_ application.
   * Wildcard directories may be specified in the following way, depending
     on the operating system and possible shell expansion:
     * `/path/to/directory/*`
     * `/path/to/directory/**`
     * `/path/to/directory/*.*`
     * `wildcard=/path/to/directory`
 * `-Xrom:(path)`
   * Specifies the SpringCoat ROM that should be used by the virtual
     machine.
 * `-Xscritchui:(ui)`
   * Rather than the default, use a specific user interface library.
   * This may also be set by using `cc.squirreljme.scritchui`.
 * `-Xsnapshot:(path-to-nps)`
   * Profile the virtual machine and store the profile results into the
     given VisualVM snapshot file.
 * `-XstartOnFirstThread`
   * Ignored.
 * `-Xthread:(single|coop|shared|multi|smt)`
   * Sets the threading mode of the virtual machine.
   * `single`/`coop` -- Single threaded using cooperative multitasking.
   * `shared` -- Single threaded using preemptive multitasking.
   * `multi`/`smt` -- Multi threaded using asynchronous multitasking.
 * `-Xtrace:option[,option[,...]]` -- Trace options
   * `all` -- Enable all.
   * `allocation` -- Allocations such as `new`.
   * `classInitialize` -- Class is initialized.
   * `defaultPackage` -- In default package only.
   * `ignoredException` -- Exception that may be ignored.
   * `instructions` -- Instructions being executed.
   * `invokeStatic` -- Static invocations.
   * `notMainThread` -- Do not trace on the main thread.
   * `methodEntry` -- Entry of methods.
   * `methodExit` -- Exit of methods.
   * `missingClass` -- Class does not exist.
   * `mleCall` -- SquirrelJME MLE Native Calls.
   * `monitorEnter` -- Monitor is entered.
   * `monitorExit` -- Exiting a monitor.
   * `monitorNotify` -- Monitor is notified.
   * `monitorWait` -- Monitor is waited on.
   * `threadNew` -- New thread is created.
   * `vmException` -- Virtual machine exceptions.
 * `-Dsysprop=value`
   * Defines the given system property. 
 * `-classpath (class:path:...)`
   * Specifies the class path to use for the current application, the entries
     must be visible from the library path.
 * `-jar (path.jar)`
   * Launches the given Jar file.
 * `-client`
   * Ignored.
 * `-server`
   * Ignored.
 * `-zero`
   * Alias for `-Xemulator:springcoat`.

## Default Directories

SquirrelJME has a set of default directories, which have a priority order
and may be overridden by environment variables.

 * `<ENVIRONMENT_VARIABLE>` -- value is read from an environment variable.
 * `[Another Type]` -- value is read from another directory type.

Any entry with {~} means the first character will be placed by your home
directory, `<HOME>` on POSIX or `<USERHOME>` on Windows.

The directory types and their order of precedence are:

 * _Cache_
   * `<SQUIRRELJME_CACHE_HOME>` {~}
   * `<LOCALAPPDATA>/squirreljme/cache` (Windows)
   * `<APPDATA>/squirreljme/cache` (Windows)
   * `<PROGRAMDATA>/squirreljme/cache` (Windows)
   * `<XDG_CACHE_HOME>/squirreljme` (POSIX)
   * `<HOME>/.cache/squirreljme` (POSIX)
 * _Config_
   * `<SQUIRRELJME_CONFIG_HOME>` {~}
   * `<APPDATA>/squirreljme/config` (Windows)
   * `<PROGRAMDATA>/squirreljme/config` (Windows)
   * `<XDG_CONFIG_HOME>/squirreljme` (POSIX)
   * `<HOME>/.config/squirreljme` (POSIX)
 * _Data_
   * `<SQUIRRELJME_DATA_HOME>` {~}
   * `<APPDATA>/squirreljme/data` (Windows)
   * `<PROGRAMDATA>/squirreljme/data` (Windows)
   * `<XDG_DATA_HOME>/squirreljme` (POSIX)
   * `<HOME>/.local/share/squirreljme` (POSIX)
 * _State_
   * `<SQUIRRELJME_STATE_HOME>` {~}
   * `<LOCALAPPDATA>/squirreljme/state` (Windows)
   * `<APPDATA>/squirreljme/state` (Windows)
   * `<PROGRAMDATA>/squirreljme/state` (Windows)
   * `<XDG_STATE_HOME>/squirreljme` (POSIX)
   * `<HOME>/.local/state/squirreljme` (POSIX)
 * _Natives_
   * `<SQUIRRELJME_LIB_JVM>` {~}
   * `<PROGRAMDATA>/squirreljme/natives` (Windows)
   * `/lib/squirreljme/natives` (POSIX)
 * _Executable_
   * This always points to the current executable, if it can be determined.
 * _Temporary_
   * `<TMP>` (Windows)
   * `<TEMP>` (POSIX)
   * `/tmp` (POSIX)
   * `[Cache]/sjme.tmp`
 * _Libraries_ -- User provided JARs are loaded from here.
   * `<SQUIRRELJME_LIBRARY_PATH>` {~}
   * `[Data]/lib`
 * _Data Bucket_ -- Non-volatile storage is here (RMS and DoJa Scratchpads).
   * `[State]/data`
 * _Extra Bucket_
   * `<SQUIRRELJME_BUCKET_EXTRA>` {~}

## Common Issues

***TRYING TO RUN DoJa/Star (i-mode) SOFTWARE?:*** Both
DoJa and Star require the accompanying `.jam` file to be able
to load the titles as the Jar has no information itself on
how to start such titles. Some DoJa/Star software will also
require the `.sp` (Scratchpad) file as it relies on DLC which
must have already been downloaded, considering the services
for downloading such DLC are no longer online.

## Framing/Scaling

SquirrelJME supports framing and scaling of any MIDP `Canvas`, to override
the default scale factor you may specify:

 * Framing -- Sets the default resolution of SquirrelJME
   * System property:
     * `cc.squirreljme.frame=WxH`
   * Environment Variable:
     * `SQUIRRELJME_FRAME=WxH`
 * Scaling -- Sets the output resolution that the frame is scaled to
   * System property:
     * `cc.squirreljme.scale=(multiplier|WxH)`
   * Environment Variable:
     * `SQUIRRELJME_SCALE=(multiplier|WxH)`

## Vendor-specific keycodes

SquirrelJME supports adjusting its input keycodes to match those of
specific vendors.

 * Vendor Keymaps -- Maps native device keys to vendor specific keys.
   * System property:
     * `cc.squirreljme.keymap=mapping`
   * Environment Variable:
     * `SQUIRRELJME_VENDOR_KEY=mapping`
   * Where `mapping` -> `cc.squirreljme.[vendorname].[device/series]`
   * The following vendor mappings are supported at this time:
     * `com.kddi`
     * `com.motorola.a1000`
     * `com.motorola` (also used by SoftBank and Alcatel devices)
     * `com.motorola.triplets` (also used by Sharp devices)
     * `com.motorola.v8` (also used by Sharp devices)
     * `com.nokia` (Also used by Samsung and Sony Ericsson)
     * `com.nokia.keyboard` (Nokia C/E-Series in numpad mode)
     * `com.sagem`
     * `com.siemens`
     * `kr.co.lge`
     * `net.rim.blackberry`
   * These properties' values are case-sensitive.
   * If a matching CSV cannot be found for the passed argument, SquirrelJME
     will fail as a result.
   * Note that the `motorola` key layout is also used by Alcatel and
     SoftBank, `motorola.triplets` is also used by Sharp devices, and
     `nokia` is also used by Sony Ericsson and Samsung devices.

## Special `file:` URIs

These are special `file:` URIs in SquirrelJME, specified by using a special
authority. Within each authority, there may be an URI encoded target, this
is specified in brackets (`<>`). Note that it is shown here as being
unencoded so that it is readable and understood. This means that if the
contained value is `<file://hello/>` this will be encoded
as `file%3A%2F%2Fhello%2F`.

 * `file://!%3Fx-squirreljme-all-volumes%3A%2F%2F%3F!/`
   * All volumes root, this lists every internal library and all
     file system roots which are available.
 * `file://!%3Fx-squirreljme-bucket%3A%2F%2F%3F!<...>/`
   * Access to the bucket storage which contains non-volatile data
     such as RMS, DoJa ScratchPads, and user installed applications.
   * `<0>` or `<data>` for the data bucket.
   * `<1>` or `<libraries>` for the library bucket.
   * `<2>` or `<extra>` for the extra bucket.
 * `file://!%3Fx-squirreljme-library%3A%2F%2F%3F!<...>/`
   * Provides access to a specific library.
   * `<...>` may be a number indicating the library index (`<0>`).
   * `<...>` may be the name of a Jar to access (`<example.jar>`).
   * The root directory may be opened as a data stream to access the raw
     library data.
 * `file://!%3Fx-squirreljme-linear-scan%3A%2F%2F%3F!<uri:...>/`
   * Walks through a binary file locating any known magic number and exposes
     a file system to access scanned content.
   * `<uri:...>` is the URI to load and access as binary data.
 * `file://!%3Fx-squirreljme-zip%3A%2F%2F%3F!<uri:...>/`
   * Provides access to reading arbitrary ZIP files.
   * `<uri:...>` is the URI to load and access as a ZIP file.

An example of referring to a library as a ZIP:

 * Start with the target library:
   * `file://!%3Fx-squirreljme-library%3A%2F%2F%3F!example.jar/`
 * URI encode the entire path:
   * `file%3A%2F%2F%21%253Fx-squirreljme-library%253A%252F%252F%253F`
     `%21example.jar%2F`
 * Then insert it into the ZIP authority:
   * `file://!%3Fx-squirreljme-zip%3A%2F%2F%3F!file%3A%2F%2F%21%253Fx-s`
     `quirreljme-library%253A%252F%252F%253F%21example.jar%2F/`

## Using the Media Player from the Command Line

The media player may be started purely from the command line with the
specified command:

 * `squirreljme -Xentry:0 -jar system-app-media-player.jar`

Note if you are on any UNIX/Posix system and are using Bash you will need
to quote URLs as Bash will try to interpret any exclamation point (`!`).

The following commands are available:

 * `list`
   * Lists the contents of the root directory.
 * `list url`
   * Lists the contents of the given URL.
 * `play url (contentType)`
   * Plays the given URL.

Listing contents will give a tab (`\t`) separated output in three columns:

 * The content type.
 * The URL of the content.
 * The file name of the content.

For example, split into three rows:

```
inode/directory
file://!%3Fx-squirreljme-library%3A%2F%2F%3F!system-app-media-player.jar/
system-app-media-player.jar/
```

Then from the second argument, to list the contents of the JAR, copy the
URL and run the list command:

 * `list file://!%3Fx-squ<TRUNCATED>yer.jar/`

This will give another list of tab (`\t`) separated columns:

```
audio/midi
file://!%3Fx-squ<TRUNCATED>yer.jar/11-Stucked-Convoi.mid
11-Stucked-Convoi.mid
```

Then to play the media, copy the URL into the given command with its
content type:

 * `play file://!%3Fx-squ<TRUNCATED>yer.jar/11-Stucked-Convoi.mid audio/midi`

If you place the media file in the data and/or library directory, which
is where non-volatile data is stored or your applications are installed, you
can then play it via:

 * `file://!%3Fx-squirreljme-bucket%3A%2F%2F%3F!data/music.mid`
 * `file://!%3Fx-squirreljme-bucket%3A%2F%2F%3F!libraries/music.mid`

While playing the output will be as followed. Progress updates will always
end in carriage return (`\r`) so that the last line is replaced. The starting
tilde (`~`) may be used to synchronize the output to find the next set of
messages to parse, while the ending caret (`^`) may be used to synchronize
the end of a line. The synchronization is mostly useful if you are parsing
the output from a script or another program, and you are not able to
differentiate between `stdout` and `stderr` as reliably.

The output template followed by an example:

```
~PlayerType: [99h 59m 59s 999999µs / 99h 59m 59s 999999µs] State (999)^
~MidiPlayer: [06s 244790µs / 04m 11s 812500µs] Started (400)^
```

States are as followed:

 * `Closed (0)`
   * The media is in the closed state.
 * `Unrealized (100)`
   * No data has yet been loaded.
 * `Realized (200)` 
   * Data for the media has been loaded.
 * `Prefetched (300)` 
   * The media and all resources are ready for playback. 
 * `Started (400)` 
   * The media is currently playing.
 * `Undefined (999)`
   * Not defined, the indicated number will be between any of the values
     indicated above.

The song will continue playing until it reaches the end.
